window.docContents[186].resolve({"/Connectivity/#section-vertex-connectivity-implies-edge-connectivity":{"contents":"Any k-vertex-connected graph is also k-edge-connected.\n\nProof sketch: given a set s of less than k edges and vertices u and v, let\ns₀ ⊆ s be those that are edges of G, since deleting the other elements of s\nchanges nothing. We then pick from each edge of s₀ other than s(u, v) an endpoint\nthat is neither u nor v, and let T be the set of chosen endpoints\n(Finset.exists_cover_avoiding). A walk that avoids a set of vertices meeting every\nedge of s₀ survives the deletion of s (Walk.reachable_deleteEdges_of_avoids).\nIf s(u, v) ∉ s₀ then T meets every edge of s₀, and a walk from u to v\navoiding T exists by k-vertex-connectivity. Otherwise T has at most k - 2\nvertices, so there is a vertex w outside T ∪ {u, v}, and we walk from u to w\navoiding T ∪ {v} and then from w to v avoiding T ∪ {u}; both of these sets\nmeet every edge of s₀, including u — v.\n\nOur first lemma says that given a finite set s of pairs s(a, b) with a ≠ b,\nthere is a set T of vertices other than u and v, with at most one vertex for\neach element of s other than s(u, v), that meets every element of s other than\ns(u, v).\n\nlemma _root_.Finset.exists_cover_avoiding [DecidableEq V] (u v : V)\n    (s : Finset (Sym2 V)) (hs : ∀ e ∈ s, ¬ e.IsDiag) :\n    ∃ T : Finset V, T.card ≤ (s.erase s(u, v)).card ∧ u ∉ T ∧ v ∉ T ∧\n      ∀ e ∈ s, e ≠ s(u, v) → ∃ x ∈ e, x ∈ T := by\n  -- We use choose! below which needs V to be nonempty.\n  have : Nonempty V := ⟨u⟩\n  have hpick : ∀ e ∈ s.erase s(u, v), ∃ x ∈ e, x ≠ u ∧ x ≠ v := by\n    intro e he\n    obtain ⟨hne, hes⟩ := Finset.mem_erase.mp he\n    have := hs e hes\n    induction e using Sym2.ind with\n    | h a b =>\n      simp only [Sym2.mk_isDiag_iff] at this\n      by_cases a = u <;> by_cases a = v <;>\n        grind [Sym2.mem_mk_left, Sym2.mem_mk_right]\n  -- Plain choose would give g : (e : Sym2 V) → e ∈ s.erase s(u, v) → V, whose\n  -- second argument is a proof of membership, so (s.erase s(u, v)).image g would\n  -- not type-check.\n  -- Using choose! removes this proof argument, giving g : Sym2 V → V\n  -- and hg : ∀ e ∈ s.erase s(u, v), g e ∈ e ∧ g e ≠ u ∧ g e ≠ v. The values of g\n  -- outside s.erase s(u, v) are arbitrary, so choose! needs Nonempty V.\n  choose! g hg using hpick\n  refine ⟨(s.erase s(u, v)).image g, Finset.card_image_le, by grind, by grind, ?_⟩\n  intro e hes hne\n  have he := Finset.mem_erase.mpr ⟨hne, hes⟩\n  exact ⟨g e, (hg e he).1, Finset.mem_image_of_mem _ he⟩\n\n\nA walk avoiding a set T of vertices that meets every edge of G in s is\nstill a walk after deleting the edges s. To move the walk into the graph\nG.deleteEdges s we use Mathlib's Walk.toDeleteEdges, which does this for any walk\nthat uses no edge of s.\n\n#check Walk.toDeleteEdges\n\n\nlemma Walk.reachable_deleteEdges_of_avoids {s : Set (Sym2 V)} {T : Finset V}\n    {x y : V} (p : G.Walk x y) (hT : ∀ e ∈ s, e ∈ G.edgeSet → ∃ z ∈ e, z ∈ T)\n    (hp : ∀ z ∈ p.support, z ∉ T) : (G.deleteEdges s).Reachable x y :=\n  ⟨p.toDeleteEdges s fun e he hes => by\n    obtain ⟨z, hze, hzT⟩ := hT e hes (p.edges_subset_edgeSet he)\n    exact hp z (p.mem_support_of_mem_edges he hze) hzT⟩\n\n\nA k-vertex-connected graph is k-edge-connected. We first reduce to the case where\nk is a natural number: a ⊤-vertex-connected graph is n-vertex-connected for\nevery n : ℕ, and by Mathlib's isEdgeConnected_top_iff_forall_nat a graph that is\nn-edge-connected for every n : ℕ is ⊤-edge-connected.\n\ntheorem IsVertexConnected.isEdgeConnected {k : ℕ∞} (hG : G.IsVertexConnected k) :\n    G.IsEdgeConnected k := by\n  classical\n  suffices h : ∀ n : ℕ, G.IsVertexConnected n → G.IsEdgeConnected n by\n    cases k with\n    | top =>\n      exact isEdgeConnected_top_iff_forall_nat.mpr fun n => h n (hG.anti le_top)\n    | coe k => exact h k hG\n  intro k hG u v s hs\n  -- s₀: the finset of edges of G that lie in s, so #s₀ < k\n  have hsfin : s.Finite :=\n    Set.encard_lt_top_iff.mp (hs.trans (ENat.natCast_lt_top k))\n  set s₀ := hsfin.toFinset.filter (· ∈ G.edgeSet)\n  have hmem {e : Sym2 V} : e ∈ s₀ ↔ e ∈ s ∧ e ∈ G.edgeSet := by simp [s₀]\n  have hs₀ : s₀.card < k := by\n    rw [hsfin.encard_eq_coe_toFinset_card] at hs\n    exact (Finset.card_filter_le _ _).trans_lt (by exact_mod_cast hs)\n  obtain ⟨T, hTcard, hTu, hTv, hT⟩ := s₀.exists_cover_avoiding u v\n    fun e he => G.not_isDiag_of_mem_edgeSet (hmem.mp he).2\n  -- a set containing T meets every edge of s₀, except possibly s(u, v)\n  have hcover (A : Finset V) (hA : T ⊆ A) (huv : s(u, v) ∈ s₀ → u ∈ A ∨ v ∈ A) :\n      ∀ e ∈ s, e ∈ G.edgeSet → ∃ x ∈ e, x ∈ A := by\n    intro e hes heG\n    by_cases he : e = s(u, v)\n    · subst he\n      rcases huv (hmem.mpr ⟨hes, heG⟩) with h | h\n      exacts [⟨u, Sym2.mem_mk_left _ _, h⟩, ⟨v, Sym2.mem_mk_right _ _, h⟩]\n    · obtain ⟨x, hx, hxT⟩ := hT e (hmem.mpr ⟨hes, heG⟩) he\n      exact ⟨x, hx, hA hxT⟩\n  by_cases huv : s(u, v) ∈ s₀\n  · -- the edge u — v is deleted, so #T ≤ k - 2\n    -- we need to find another vertex w outside T ∪ {u, v}\n    have hne : u ≠ v := (G.mem_edgeSet.mp (hmem.mp huv).2).ne\n    have hTk : T.card + 2 ≤ k := by grind\n    have hins (a : V) : (insert a T).card ≤ T.card + 1 := Finset.card_insert_le _ _\n    obtain ⟨w, hw⟩ := hG.exists_notMem (insert u (insert v T))\n      (Nat.cast_le.mpr (by grind))\n    simp only [Finset.mem_insert, not_or] at hw\n    obtain ⟨hwu, hwv, hwT⟩ := hw\n    -- we can walk from u to w avoiding T ∪ {v}\n    obtain ⟨p₁, hp₁⟩ := hG.exists_walk (insert v T)\n      (Nat.cast_lt.mpr (by grind)) u w (by grind) (by grind)\n    -- we can walk from w to v avoiding T ∪ {u}\n    obtain ⟨p₂, hp₂⟩ := hG.exists_walk (insert u T)\n      (Nat.cast_lt.mpr (by grind)) w v (by grind) (by grind)\n    exact (p₁.reachable_deleteEdges_of_avoids (hcover _ (Finset.subset_insert _ _)\n        fun _ => .inr (Finset.mem_insert_self _ _)) hp₁).trans\n      (p₂.reachable_deleteEdges_of_avoids (hcover _ (Finset.subset_insert _ _)\n        fun _ => .inl (Finset.mem_insert_self _ _)) hp₂)\n  · -- otherwise T meets every edge of s₀\n    have := Finset.card_erase_le (s := s₀) (a := s(u, v))\n    obtain ⟨p, hp⟩ := hG.exists_walk T (Nat.cast_lt.mpr (by grind)) u v hTu hTv\n    exact p.reachable_deleteEdges_of_avoids (hcover T subset_rfl (absurd · huv)) hp\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity\u0009Edge and Vertex Connectivity","header":"10.4.3. Vertex Connectivity Implies Edge Connectivity","id":"/Connectivity/#section-vertex-connectivity-implies-edge-connectivity"},"/Constructions/#section-mycielskian":{"contents":"The Mycielskian of a graph G : SimpleGraph V is constructed as follows. Its\nvertex set consists of two disjoint copies Vₗ and Vᵣ of V together with a new\napex vertex which we call none.\n\nThe edges inside Vₗ are identical to those of G; there are no edges inside Vᵣ;\nthere is an edge between vertices a ∈ Vₗ and b ∈ Vᵣ iff there is an edge in G\nbetween the vertices of which a and b are copies. Finally none is adjacent to\nevery vertex in Vᵣ but to no vertices in Vₗ.\n\nThis construction is due to Jan Mycielski J. Mycielski, Sur le coloriage des graphs [On the colouring of graphs], Colloq. Math. 3 (1955), no. 2, 161–162., who showed that:\n\n1. if G is triangle-free, then mμ[G] is also triangle-free.2. if G has chromatic number k, then mμ[G] has chromatic number k + 1.\n\nThus starting with any triangle-free graph we can use it to construct triangle-free\ngraphs with arbitrarily large chromatic number.\n\nIn order to define the Mycielskian of a graph G in Lean we need to first describe\nits vertex type. Given that it is built from two disjoint copies of the vertex type\nof G together with a new vertex, we can use Option (V ⊕ V). (We already saw the\nSum type ⊕ for completeBipartiteGraph and Option is the natural way to extend\na type with a new element called none.)\n\nsection mycielskian_section\nvariable {V : Type*}\n\n#source Option\n\n\nThe Mycielskian of a graph G./-- The *Mycielskian* of a graph G. -/\n@[simps]\ndef mycielskian (G : SimpleGraph V) : SimpleGraph (Option (V ⊕ V)) where\n  Adj x y := match x, y with\n    | some (Sum.inl v), some (Sum.inl w) => G.Adj v w\n    | some (Sum.inl v), some (Sum.inr w) => G.Adj v w\n    | some (Sum.inr v), some (Sum.inl w) => G.Adj v w\n    | none, some (Sum.inr w) => True\n    | some (Sum.inr v), none => True\n    | _, _ => False\n  symm := ⟨by rintro (_ | _ | _) (_ | _ | _) h <;> simp_all [Adj.symm]⟩\n  loopless := ⟨by rintro (_ | _ | _) <;> simp⟩\n\n\nWe introduce the notation mμ[G] for the Mycielskian of a graph G.\n\n@[inherit_doc mycielskian]\nscoped notation \"mμ[\" G \"]\" => mycielskian G\n\n\nAs mentioned above if we iterate the Mycielskian construction starting with a\ntriangle-free graph then we obtain a sequence of triangle-free graphs with increasing\nchromatic number.\n\nIn particular we can start with K[2]. Its Mycielskian is isomorphic to the\n5-cycle C[5] (see Vertex-Transitivity of Mycielskians),\nand the Mycielskian of C[5] is called the Grötzsch graph H. Grötzsch, Zur Theorie der diskreten Gebilde, VII: Ein Dreifarbensatz für dreikreisfreie Netze auf der Kugel [On the theory of discrete structures, VII: A three-colour theorem for triangle-free networks on the sphere], Wiss. Z. Martin-Luther-Univ. Halle-Wittenberg Math.-Natur. Reihe 8 (1959), 109–120..\n\nThe Grötzsch graph (i.e. the Mycielskian of C[5])./-- The *Grötzsch graph* (i.e. the Mycielskian of C[5]). -/\n@[simps!]\nabbrev grotzschGraph : SimpleGraph (Option (Fin 5 ⊕ Fin 5)) := mμ[C[5]]\n\n\n#check grotzschGraph_adj\n\n\nThe Grötzsch graph (i.e. the Mycielskian of C_5)\n\nIn order to talk about the vertices of mμ[G] it will be useful to introduce some\nterminology. We name the two copies of V in a mycielskian namespace, since these\nnames only make sense for this construction. The @[match_pattern] attribute lets us\nuse them (and the notation below) as patterns when defining a function by pattern\nmatching, just like the constructors none, some, Sum.inl and Sum.inr.\n\nA copy of the vertex v in the \"left\" part of the Mycielskian.namespace mycielskian\n\n/-- A copy of the vertex v in the \"left\" part of the Mycielskian. -/\n@[match_pattern]\nabbrev left (v : V) : Option (V ⊕ V) := some (Sum.inl v)\n\n\nA copy of the vertex v in the \"right\" part of the Mycielskian./-- A copy of the vertex v in the \"right\" part of the Mycielskian. -/\n@[match_pattern]\nabbrev right (v : V) : Option (V ⊕ V) := some (Sum.inr v)\n\nend mycielskian\n\n\nWe can introduce notation for these as follows. The left copy of v : V is denoted\nv ₗ and the right copy is denoted v ᵣ.\n\n@[inherit_doc mycielskian.left]\nscoped postfix:max \"ₗ\" => mycielskian.left\n@[inherit_doc mycielskian.right]\nscoped postfix:max \"ᵣ\" => mycielskian.right\n\n\nThe @[simps] attribute on our definition of mycielskian generated a @[simp]\nlemma mycielskian_adj, which rewrites mμ[G].Adj x y to the match in its\ndefinition. Once both vertices are named, as in v ₗ, w ᵣ or none, simp\nreduces the match, so it (and tactics such as aesop that call it) can decide any\nadjacency between named vertices.\n\nexample {G : SimpleGraph V} (v w : V) : mμ[G].Adj (v ₗ) (w ᵣ) ↔ G.Adj v w := by simp\n\n\nexample {G : SimpleGraph V} (v w : V) : ¬ mμ[G].Adj (v ᵣ) (w ᵣ) := by simp\n\n\nWe now prove a few lemmas about the Mycielskian construction that will be useful\nlater on.\n\nThe maps v ↦ v ₗ and v ↦ v ᵣ are injective. (In the proofs, e |>.comp f means\n(e).comp f: the |>. lets us use dot notation on the result of\nOption.some_injective _ without extra brackets.)\n\nlemma mycielskian.left_injective : Function.Injective (fun v : V => v ₗ) :=\n  Option.some_injective _ |>.comp Sum.inl_injective\n\n\nlemma mycielskian.right_injective : Function.Injective (fun v : V => v ᵣ) :=\n  Option.some_injective _ |>.comp Sum.inr_injective\n\n\nThe neighbourhoods of the vertices in mμ[G] are as follows.\n\nA left copy v ₗ is adjacent to the left and right copies of the neighbours of v\nin G.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma neighborSet_mycielskian_left {G : SimpleGraph V} (v : V) :\n    mμ[G].neighborSet (v ₗ) = (G.neighborSet v).image (· ₗ) ∪\n      (G.neighborSet v).image (· ᵣ) := by\n  ext x\n  grind [mem_neighborSet, mycielskian_adj]\n\n\nA right copy v ᵣ is adjacent to the left copies of the neighbours of v in G\nand to none.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma neighborSet_mycielskian_right {G : SimpleGraph V} (v : V) :\n    mμ[G].neighborSet (v ᵣ) = insert none ((G.neighborSet v).image (· ₗ)) := by\n  ext x\n  grind [mem_neighborSet, mycielskian_adj]\n\n\nThe apex vertex none is adjacent to the right copies of all vertices in G.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma neighborSet_mycielskian_none {G : SimpleGraph V} :\n    mμ[G].neighborSet none = Set.range (· ᵣ) := by\n  ext x\n  grind [mem_neighborSet, mycielskian_adj]\n\n\nThe left copies, the right copies and none are pairwise disjoint.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma mycielskian.disjoint_image_left_image_right (s t : Set V) :\n    Disjoint (s.image (· ₗ)) (t.image (· ᵣ)) := by\n  grind\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma mycielskian.disjoint_image_left_none (s : Set V) :\n    Disjoint (s.image (· ₗ)) {none} := by\n  grind\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma mycielskian.disjoint_image_right_none (s : Set V) :\n    Disjoint (s.image (· ᵣ)) {none} := by\n  grind\n\nend mycielskian_section\n\n\nAs mentioned above we will be interested in iterating the Mycielskian construction,\nbut in order to do this we first need to define the iterated vertex type.Below\nwe use Type _ which means that the universe level is inferred (it will be the same\nas the universe level of V). If you replaced Type _ with Type*, the definition\nwould need to change.\n\nAs with HypercubeVert, the @[implicit_reducible] attribute lets Lean unfold\nmμᵥ^(n + 1) V when checking implicit and instance-implicit arguments.\n\nThe vertex type of the n-th Mycielskian of a SimpleGraph V (denoted\nmμᵥ^n V)./-- The vertex type of the n-th Mycielskian of a SimpleGraph V (denoted\nmμᵥ^n V). -/\n@[implicit_reducible]\ndef MycielskianVert (V : Type*) : ℕ → Type _\n  | 0     => V\n  | n + 1 => Option (MycielskianVert V n ⊕ MycielskianVert V n)\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma mycielskianVert_zero {V : Type*} : MycielskianVert V 0 = V := rfl\n\n\nlemma mycielskianVert_succ {V : Type*} (n : ℕ) :\n    MycielskianVert V (n + 1) =\n      Option (MycielskianVert V n ⊕ MycielskianVert V n) := rfl\n\n\nNotation for the vertex type of the n-th Mycielskian. Unlike notation, a syntax\ndeclaration only tells Lean how to parse mμᵥ^n V. For Lean to also print\nMycielskianVert V n as mμᵥ^n V (in goals, hovers and #check output) we add an\nunexpander, which turns the application back into the notation.\n\n@[inherit_doc MycielskianVert]\nscoped syntax \"mμᵥ^\" term:max ppSpace term:max : term\nscoped macro_rules\n  | `(mμᵥ^ $n $V) => `(MycielskianVert $V $n)\n\n\nPrint MycielskianVert V n as mμᵥ^n V./-- Print MycielskianVert V n as mμᵥ^n V. -/\n@[scoped app_unexpander MycielskianVert]\nprivate def unexpandMycielskianVert : Lean.PrettyPrinter.Unexpander\n  | `($_ $V $n) => `(mμᵥ^ $n $V)\n  | _ => throw ()\n\n\nThe n-th iterated Mycielskian of a graph G (denoted mμ^n G)./-- The n-th iterated Mycielskian of a graph G (denoted mμ^n G). -/\ndef iterMycielskian {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) : (n : ℕ) → SimpleGraph (mμᵥ^n V)\n  | 0     => G\n  | n + 1 => mμ[iterMycielskian G n]\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma iterMycielskian_zero {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) :\n    iterMycielskian G 0 = G := rfl\n\n\nlemma iterMycielskian_succ {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) (n : ℕ) :\n    iterMycielskian G (n + 1) = mμ[iterMycielskian G n] := rfl\n\n\nNotation for the iterated Mycielskian, again with an unexpander so that Lean prints\niterMycielskian G n as mμ^n G.\n\n@[inherit_doc iterMycielskian]\nscoped syntax \"mμ^\" term:max ppSpace term:max : term\nscoped macro_rules\n  | `(mμ^ $n $G) => `(iterMycielskian $G $n)\n\n\nPrint iterMycielskian G n as mμ^n G./-- Print iterMycielskian G n as mμ^n G. -/\n@[scoped app_unexpander iterMycielskian]\nprivate def unexpandIterMycielskian : Lean.PrettyPrinter.Unexpander\n  | `($_ $G $n) => `(mμ^ $n $G)\n  | _ => throw ()\n\n\n#check mμ^3 C[5]\n\n\nThe postfix operators ₗ and ᵣ can be stacked: (0 : Fin 5)ₗᵣᵣ means ((0ₗ)ᵣ)ᵣ,\nthe right copy of the right copy of the left copy of 0, which is a vertex of the\n3rd iterated Mycielskian of C[5]. Written out in full this would be the unreadable\nterm some (Sum.inr (some (Sum.inr (some (Sum.inl 0))))). The adjacency lemmas above\npeel off one layer at a time: left–left and right–left pairs are adjacent exactly\nwhen the underlying vertices are, so (0 : Fin 5)ₗᵣᵣ is adjacent to (1 : Fin 5)ₗₗₗ\nbecause 0 and 1 are adjacent in C[5]. In the proof, simpa [lemmas] using e\nsimplifies both the goal and the type of e, then closes the goal with e.\n\nexample : (mμ^3 C[5]).Adj (0 : Fin 5)ₗᵣᵣ (1 : Fin 5)ₗₗₗ := by\n  simpa [iterMycielskian_succ] using (by decide : C[5].Adj 0 1)\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions","header":"3.5. Mycielskian","id":"/Constructions/#section-mycielskian"}});